REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w11","title":"Notes, business letters, instructions, data reports and essays","summary":"Eight lectures on practical writing. Plan your points first, keep only the facts the reader needs, put them in a fixed order with the most important first, and write short, simple sentences. Each lecture shows this on a real task (a note on a phone, two business letters, directions for a metro ride, two data reports, an essay), from a weak first draft to a better final draft.","n":11,"lectures":[{"code":"11.1","title":"Note-writing | techniques for clear, concise & organized notes","video":"dObw3Ikv1FE","seconds":1543},{"code":"11.2","title":"Business writing | clarity, precision & structure for professional communication","video":"tm2Ou96Kajs","seconds":1517},{"code":"11.3","title":"Writing instructions","video":"eZYAswxzk7w","seconds":1778},{"code":"11.4","title":"Business writing- sample | blending professionalism & personal context in letters","video":"IDpOwgM3aZo","seconds":1629},{"code":"11.5","title":"Data interpretation (visual to verbal) | turning charts & tables into clear writing","video":"9kiv6xUsQsQ","seconds":1474},{"code":"11.6","title":"Data interpretation sample- a household survey","video":"Lldu_p3qVcI","seconds":1576},{"code":"11.7","title":"Writing Essay","video":"-qpN5ilfDy8","seconds":1377},{"code":"11.8","title":"Power of simplicity - words & sentences | clear, impactful & accessible writing","video":"9nsKHDgllW0","seconds":1900}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"note-writing","level":2,"title":"Note-writing"},{"id":"the-five-points-of-a-note","level":3,"title":"The five points of a note"},{"id":"first-draft-and-final-draft","level":3,"title":"First draft and final draft"},{"id":"accuracy-the-one-right-word","level":3,"title":"Accuracy: the one right word"},{"id":"business-writing-facts-form-and-order","level":2,"title":"Business writing: facts, form and order"},{"id":"the-lost-bag-the-task","level":3,"title":"The lost bag: the task"},{"id":"the-first-draft-278-words-few-facts","level":3,"title":"The first draft: 278 words, few facts"},{"id":"the-final-draft-154-words-all-facts","level":3,"title":"The final draft: 154 words, all facts"},{"id":"writing-instructions-maps-and-plans","level":2,"title":"Writing instructions: maps and plans"},{"id":"the-metro-task","level":3,"title":"The metro task"},{"id":"the-first-draft-and-the-comments","level":3,"title":"The first draft and the comments"},{"id":"the-final-draft-numbered-steps","level":3,"title":"The final draft: numbered steps"},{"id":"personal-details-in-a-business-letter","level":2,"title":"Personal details in a business letter"},{"id":"the-task-a-trip-to-blackpool","level":3,"title":"The task: a trip to Blackpool"},{"id":"the-rough-draft-243-words","level":3,"title":"The rough draft: 243 words"},{"id":"the-final-draft-152-words","level":3,"title":"The final draft: 152 words"},{"id":"data-interpretation-from-visual-to-verbal","level":2,"title":"Data interpretation: from visual to verbal"},{"id":"what-a-data-report-must-say","level":3,"title":"What a data report must say"},{"id":"the-facebook-table","level":3,"title":"The Facebook table"},{"id":"a-data-report-the-household-survey","level":2,"title":"A data report: the household survey"},{"id":"the-task-and-the-chart","level":3,"title":"The task and the chart"},{"id":"the-first-draft-copied-numbers-and-a-tangent","level":3,"title":"The first draft: copied numbers and a tangent"},{"id":"the-final-draft-insight-not-a-copy","level":3,"title":"The final draft: insight, not a copy"},{"id":"writing-an-essay","level":2,"title":"Writing an essay"},{"id":"the-task-agriculture-in-india","level":3,"title":"The task: agriculture in India"},{"id":"the-first-draft-594-words","level":3,"title":"The first draft: 594 words"},{"id":"vague-words-cliche-s-and-jargon","level":3,"title":"Vague words, clichés and jargon"},{"id":"the-final-draft-252-words","level":3,"title":"The final draft: 252 words"},{"id":"the-power-of-simplicity","level":2,"title":"The power of simplicity"},{"id":"shakespeare-the-opening-of-macbeth","level":3,"title":"Shakespeare: the opening of Macbeth"},{"id":"wordsworth-composed-upon-westminster-bridge","level":3,"title":"Wordsworth: “Composed upon Westminster Bridge”"},{"id":"dickens-and-hawking","level":3,"title":"Dickens and Hawking"},{"id":"friends-and-the-public","level":3,"title":"Friends and the public"},{"id":"how-to-write-simple-sentences","level":3,"title":"How to write simple sentences"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Note-writing</strong> (11.1): a note on something you own, in about 150 words. Plan a <strong>synopsis</strong> first: when you got it, what it looks like, its special features, what you like best, any event linked to it. Put everything in the first draft; keep “all and only” the needed facts in the final draft (the phone note: 385 words in 21 sentences became 150 words in 12). Choose the <strong>accurate</strong> word: a screen is a <em>part</em>, not an <em>extra</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Business writing</strong> (11.2): it is full of <strong>facts</strong> (what, when, where, how much, how many), it has a <strong>fixed form</strong> (“a place for everything”), and it follows a <strong>rank order</strong> (the most important thing first). The lost-bag letter: a first draft of 278 words full of feelings gives almost no facts; the final draft (154 words) names the bus, the time, the stops, the bag and what is inside it.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing instructions</strong> (11.3): maps and plans need language for <strong>sequence, directions, numbers and options</strong>. A mistake here is not forgiven like a slip of grammar. The metro letter: cut the chat; give the ticket and the <strong>token</strong>, the line colours, where to change, the time, and the exit number.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal details in a business letter</strong> (11.4): ask your host <strong>in advance</strong>, in a business-like, formal and polite way. The first sentence says where, how and when you arrive: “I will reach Blackpool by the 4:30 train from London on 17 March.”</li>\n<li><strong>Data interpretation, visual to verbal</strong> (11.5): numbers make writing <strong>objective</strong>. A report on a table or chart says what it is about, how it is presented, the main trends, the significant details, the differences between points, and a conclusion, in about 150 words. Do not copy every number: find the trends.</li>\n<li><strong>A household survey</strong> (11.6): the same plan for a pie chart. The first draft copies the numbers and goes off on a <strong>tangent</strong>; the final draft says what the chart does not tell, uses chunks (“over half”, “one fourth”) and ends with a conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing an essay</strong> (11.7): an essay tests your language <strong>and</strong> your knowledge of the world and of what matters most. It needs <strong>content, planning, examples and evidence</strong>. The agriculture essay went from 594 words to 252 by cutting <strong>irrelevant ideas</strong>, <strong>vague words, clichés and jargon</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The power of simplicity</strong> (11.8): great writers (Shakespeare, Wordsworth, Dickens, Hawking) use simple words and sentences. Five rules: sentences of <strong>at most 10 words</strong>, <strong>one verb</strong> in each, words of <strong>one or two syllables</strong>, <strong>more than one draft</strong>, and a <strong>dictionary and thesaurus</strong> when in doubt.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"note-writing\">Note-writing</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>All eight lectures of this week are by Dr Shreesh Chaudhary (retired professor, Humanities and Social Sciences, IIT Madras). Lecture 11.1 starts from a problem every writer has: limits. “No matter how accomplished a writer, each writer has to write within limited space, limited time, limited number of words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The danger is not that we know too little. When we write about something we know well, such as ourselves in a <a href=\"#/english1/w10/bio-notes\">bio-note</a>, we know so much that we want to say too much (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). This lecture’s subject is of the same kind, an object you own and know well: a pen, a phone, a car, a book, a piece of furniture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Note (in this lecture)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A short piece of writing, here about 150 words, that describes one thing <strong>factually</strong>: what it is and what it is like, not a story of your feelings about it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The task slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=138s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:18</a>): “In about 150 words, write a note on anything you own, describing all its features. Hint: when bought - physical description - colour, shape, size, etc. - any special features - what you like best about it - any associated event/incident”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synopsis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The list of points you will cover, made <strong>before</strong> you write: “the highlights of points”, jotted in “a private note, in a rough sketch” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). You might like to write a book about your phone, but “no reader has so much time” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). So you choose the points first.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-five-points-of-a-note\">The five points of a note</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point</th>\n<th>Ask yourself</th>\n<th>From the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Date of association</td>\n<td>When did it come to you, or to your family?</td>\n<td>the day, month and year (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical description</td>\n<td>Soft or hard? Black or white? Tall or short? Heavy or light? Colour, shape, size?</td>\n<td>“it is so many inches long” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special features</td>\n<td>What makes it unique? Why did you choose it?</td>\n<td>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you like best</td>\n<td>What do you like most? What do you not like?</td>\n<td>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>An event or incident</td>\n<td>What happened with it that you will always remember? If it is gone, why?</td>\n<td>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“That will easily give you about 150 words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same plan for a meal and a house (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A meal, physical description:</strong> grains of rice long or short, thin or thick, whole or broken; rice white, red, green or yellow; chapati soft or hard (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Special feature:</strong> some rice has an <em>aroma</em> (a pleasant smell); some has none (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>What people like best:</strong> some like the red rice of Kerala because “it is easy to digest” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A house, what you like best:</strong> “the sun rising in my veranda every morning”, or “sun setting in my balcony every evening” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>An associated event:</strong> “my parents were married in this house”; “my grandparents built it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>); a family house where the first Prime Minister, Jawaharlal Nehru, once came for lunch (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “every physical object has some special unique quality: describe that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A disposable society</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>We do not keep things for long now. Once a fountain pen passed from grandfather to father to son; today we change our things often (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). So the “event” point can also say why the object is no longer with you (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"first-draft-and-final-draft\">First draft and final draft</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Draft</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>One version of a piece of writing. “It’s rarely the case that your first draft is a perfect draft”: a good writer or speaker is “mentally prepared to do several drafts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First draft:</strong> “write anything that comes to your head, get everything down on paper”.</li>\n<li><strong>Then edit:</strong> decide what is fit to be said, and cut the rest (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same method for a speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Before you speak in public, “tell everything to your recorder”, then edit. Then “it is highly unlikely that you will make a mistake” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The activity slide asks you to compare the two drafts of a note on three things: the <strong>number of words</strong>, the <strong>number of sentences</strong>, and the <strong>content</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=626s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:26</a>). Content means: does the note give a physical description, the date, the parts, the colour, and what the user liked or disliked (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)?</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">My cell phone, first draft (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=744s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:24</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>It begins: “I have a Tellmi Note 6 Pro handset for my cell phone. I bought it last year for Rs 11,000/- only. It has 4GB of RAM memory, but it can be expanded up to 256GB, which makes it as good as a medium-class laptop computer. For such a powerful phone, it has a rather small display screen, only about 6.26 inches.”</p>\n<p>Four more paragraphs follow (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=776s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:56</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=786s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:06</a>): the cameras (“a whopping 20MP”), the battery (“without any worries for up to three days, or a train journey across India”), an earlier “yPhone” and the writer’s worries about charging it, the weight, and the dual SIM (“And enjoying this power.”). <strong>385 words.</strong></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">My cell phone, final version (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=790s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“I have a Tellmi Note 6 handset. I bought it last year for Rs 11,000/- only. It has 4GB RAM memory. But it has only a 6.26 inch display screen. Its dual camera, with a 12/5MP in the rear, and 20/2MP in front, can cover large spaces. But I like it more for its battery, it is above 4,000mAh. Without recharging, I use it for up to three days. Its processor is also good. Earlier I had an yPhone, with a more powerful processor, but smaller power back up. I used to charge it twice daily. But at about 300g, it is rather heavy. The best thing here, however, is its own link to the Net, getting and sending mails and messages anytime anywhere. This set has slots for two sim cards, in one I keep my business account, the other has my friends and family.” <strong>150 words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=862s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:22</a>).</p>\n<p>It keeps the facts that matter: when (last year), the physical details (memory, screen, weight), the special features (camera, battery, two SIM slots), what the writer likes best (the battery; “the best thing” is the link to the Net), and a comparison with the earlier phone. (This reading is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The answers slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=896s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:56</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of words</td>\n<td>385</td>\n<td>150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of sentences</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What the numbers show (my arithmetic, from the slide):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Words cut: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">385</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">235</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Share cut: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">385</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">235</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.61</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> so about 6 words in every 10 went.</li>\n<li>Words per sentence, first draft: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">385</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">18.3</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>Words per sentence, final draft: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12.5</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the final draft has fewer sentences, and shorter ones.</p>\n<p>The slide that puts the two drafts side by side (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=946s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:46</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“I have a Tellmi Note 6 Pro handset for my cell phone. I bought it last year for Rs 11,000/- It has a 4GB RAM memory, but it can be expanded upto 256GB, which makes it as good as a medium class laptop computer.”</td>\n<td>“I have a Tellmi Note 6 handset. I bought it last year for Rs 11,000/- only. It has 4GB RAM memory.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“For such a powerful phone, it has a rather small display screen, only about 6.26 inches. For young people, however, that is not a problem.”</td>\n<td>“But it has only a 6.26 inch display screen.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“For such a powerful phone, it has a rather small display screen ...”: “do we need to see all that?” All those words say one fact: the screen is 6.26 inches (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). When you write to convey information, “rather than writing a novel or a short story about it, it is better to be factual and only factual and nothing but factual” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Redundant, verbose, “all and only”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Redundant</strong> words repeat what is already said, or add nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Verbose</strong> means using more words than you need. Many learners of English in India and other Asian countries “tend to become verbose” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The final draft “must contain <strong>all and only</strong>” the words “that are essential”: nothing needed is missing, and nothing else is there (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"accuracy-the-one-right-word\">Accuracy: the one right word</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many words may fit a place (<em>nice</em>, <em>marvellous</em>, <em>good</em>), “but only one of them will be accurate” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). Think of a peg and a hole: if the peg is too big, it will not go in; if it is too small, it goes in but will not stay (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Or of containers: milk is best drunk from a tumbler or a cup, but a cake does not go in a cup, unless it is a cupcake (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Inaccurate / Accurate” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1118s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:38</a>), with the lecturer’s reasons:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Inaccurate (first draft)</th>\n<th>Accurate</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“...can be expanded up to”</td>\n<td>“Can expand to”</td>\n<td>no need for the passive <em>be expanded</em>: “it can expand” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...phones that require frequent charging for phones...”</td>\n<td>“...need frequent charging for power...”</td>\n<td>“phones ... for phones” says the same word twice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“more powerful processor, but very small power back up”</td>\n<td>“more powerful processor, but smaller power back up”</td>\n<td>it compares two phones: the old phone’s backup was <strong>smaller</strong> than the new one’s (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“I do not prefer phones that require...”</td>\n<td>prefer → like</td>\n<td><em>prefer</em> compares two things, <em>prefer A to B</em> (<em>I prefer tea</em> is also correct, with the other choice understood); here you mean <em>I do not like</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...using it without any worries...”</td>\n<td>“...without worrying about its battery...”</td>\n<td>a phone cannot take away all your worries; say which worry (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“other extras”</td>\n<td>“parts”</td>\n<td>a screen or a battery is a part of the phone, not an extra (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...in pant pockets...”</td>\n<td>“a pant pocket / in a pocket in pant”</td>\n<td>one phone cannot be in two pockets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Carried away by feeling</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Quite often while writing we get carried away by the force of our own emotion and we make a mistake” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>). An inaccurate word is not just a small slip: “it won’t get you higher marks” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learn the principle, not the answer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many parts of language follow “very mechanical principles”: once you see the rule, you can go on without mistakes. The other way, learning things by heart to pass an exam, leaves you with knowledge but no confidence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"business-writing-facts-form-and-order\">Business writing: facts, form and order</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.2. Learning a language is learning a <strong>skill</strong>. You learn it by doing it, above all by writing and speaking. You will make mistakes, and “through those mistakes lies the highway to learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Business writing makes two special demands: it is full of <strong>facts</strong>, and it keeps a <strong>fixed order</strong>, both in its form and in its contents (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=118s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:58</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Factual details</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Most business writings contain a great deal of factual details, like what, when, where, how much, for how much and how many, etc.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=118s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:58</a>): the goods and their kinds, the quantities, the prices, and offers such as “if you buy so much, then you pay so much” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fixed form</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Business writing should follow the form strictly. It helps the reader. They expect one thing in one place and another thing in another place” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=252s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:12</a>). In short: “there is a place for everything and everything has a place” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rank order</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Contents must also be ordered sequentially. So a rank order should be followed, most important thing from the point of view of communication first, least important last” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=578s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:38</a>). In the lecturer’s words: “important things first, less important next and still less important things last” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Pictures from the lecture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>At home:</strong> books in one place, clothes in another, shoes in another, vessels and dishes in another (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=252s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:12</a>). Even in a small home each corner has its job: one for puja, one for dining, one for rest, one for storing things (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In a library:</strong> “a misplaced book is a lost book” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In a kitchen:</strong> the pantry and the laundry are not mixed; spoons, dishes, vessels and ladles each have a fixed place, so things are easy to find, store and keep (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:20</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In an operation theatre:</strong> the nurse knows which tool the surgeon needs next; the surgeon just holds out a hand (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Art (poetry, painting, music)</th>\n<th>Business, science, engineering, academic writing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Order</td>\n<td>may be broken on purpose</td>\n<td>“the order cannot be broken” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Form</td>\n<td>free: a painted demon may have “hands where feet are” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</td>\n<td>fixed: do not change it “unnecessarily” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=578s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:38</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">What a broken order costs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Your family and friends will read you anyway, because they love you. A business reader gets confused and decides: “if this person’s communication skill is so poor, what kind of a business executive or firm this is” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). The result is “loss and confusion and waste of time, energy and resources” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). With the wrong order, a reader may not even know what to do first: “should I first send flowers, or should I first send this consignment of Covaxin medicines?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lost-bag-the-task\">The lost bag: the task</h3>\n<p>This is “a very frequent proficiency test question” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:40</a>): “Within about a 150 words, write a letter to the manager of the bus company requesting him to help you find the bag you left behind on a bus of his company yesterday.”</p>\n<p>Why not just write “Dear manager, I left my bag on your bus yesterday. If you have found it, please return it to me. Here is my address”? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A big bus company carries hundreds of passengers every day.</li>\n<li>If even one per cent of them leave something behind, its lost-and-found office may hold “two to three dozen things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Bags are among the things passengers leave behind most, with umbrellas, cell phones, books, pens, purses and wallets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So the office must know <strong>which</strong> bag is yours.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The form of the letter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the order of the lecture’s example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>From:</strong> the writer and her address, here as a letterhead: “Mira Lobo Prabhu, No. 31, Coondapur Road, Mangalore” (a made-up name).</li>\n<li><strong>To:</strong> “The Manager, Mangalagiri Transport Company, Railway Station Road, Mangalore”.</li>\n<li><strong>Date:</strong> “20 Jan, 2021”.</li>\n<li><strong>Salutation</strong> (the greeting): “Sir,” (or “Sir/Madam”).</li>\n<li><strong>Subject line:</strong> “Sub: Left Luggage - Reg.” (<em>Sub</em> = subject; <em>Reg.</em> = regarding.)</li>\n<li><strong>The body</strong>, then the closing (“With best regards”, “Yours faithfully”) and the name.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Subject before or after “Sir”?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some organisations put the subject line before the salutation. “Do it the way your organisation does”: local rules must be followed, just as cars keep to the right in America but to the left in Britain and India (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-draft-278-words-few-facts\">The first draft: 278 words, few facts</h3>\n<p>The first draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=844s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:04</a>) “begins on an emotional note”. It has 278 words, “nearly 128 words more than required” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>): <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">278</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">128</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The first draft says</th>\n<th>Why it does not help</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“I had an old and very precious foam leather bag that my mother had given me at the time of my wedding some seven years ago”</td>\n<td>The bag carries no label saying “happy wedding”. This belongs in an autobiography, not in a business letter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“That bag is very dear to my heart, like my life you can say. I have had a sleepless night”</td>\n<td>“The reader can pity you”, “but that will not help the reader find the bag quickly” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1104s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:24</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a long story: talking to a fellow passenger, the conductor calling “Bank Stop, Bank Stop”, phoning her friend from her grandmother’s phone</td>\n<td>It tells “how the bag has been lost, but it gives no particular of the bag” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“It has my debit card, my phone, my company id card, some cash, and a few lady’s items.”</td>\n<td>Which card? Which phone? Which company? How much cash? What “lady’s items”: “clothes or cosmetics”? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“The bag is blue colour, and it has long strap.”</td>\n<td>almost the only description of the bag</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Correct is not the same as effective</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The writer “makes nearly no mistake of either grammar or punctuation or vocabulary”, yet almost the whole page is wasted. She “apparently writes well but does not know how to write effectively” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-final-draft-154-words-all-facts\">The final draft: 154 words, all facts</h3>\n<p>The final draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1256s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:56</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1316s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:56</a>) keeps the same form; the company’s address now has its PIN code, “Mangalore 570 014”. The lecturer does not call it a model answer, “but maybe this is acceptable, and this has come after several drafts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What the final draft says</th>\n<th>The question it answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The journey</td>\n<td>“I boarded 7 T, at about 05:35 pm at the Water Tank stop on the Kutchehri Road, and left it at the Corporation Bank Stop on the Sundara Street.”</td>\n<td>Which bus? When? Where did you get on, and where off? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The request</td>\n<td>“But while leaving the bus, I forgot to pick up my bag. Please, be good enough to help me find it.”</td>\n<td>What do you want?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The bag</td>\n<td>“This is a blue foam leather lady’s bag. It has two brass clips, besides two side pockets with zips.” A name, “Parke Smith”, can be seen on it.</td>\n<td>Colour, material, fittings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A phone inside</td>\n<td>“The bag has a red Nokya 512 Cell Phone”, and the letter gives the number to call it on</td>\n<td>How can the finder check?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The contents</td>\n<td>“1. Camera Bank debit card, with my name. MIRA LOBO PRABHU; 2. Export Promotion Council’s ID card with my name; 3. A small pocket note book, bearing my name and address, 4. A pink shade lipstick bearing ‘La Femme’. 5. Side pockets have business cards, mine and also others’.”</td>\n<td>What exactly is inside?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The close</td>\n<td>“Please, kindly help. With best regards, M L Prabhu”</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “if the bag has not been stolen, anyone can, using this description, find the bag”: easy for the finder, and easy for the owner (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). In business writing, follow the order of importance strictly, and all the rules of format.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise a little every day</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Write something every day, “only 50 words, only 10 words”: a birthday greeting, a slogan for a product, the end of a witty proverb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). No teacher can give you a tablet to take with breakfast so that “by evening you have got language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). Check words in the dictionary on your phone and change what is not good. But changes come only after you write a first draft, “even if wrongly” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The activity slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=1386s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:06</a>), each in about 150 words: (1) a birthday greeting to a friend; (2) a letter to the Bus Depot Manager, Mahabalipuram, asking him to help you find the wallet you left behind on a bus of his company yesterday.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-instructions-maps-and-plans\">Writing instructions: maps and plans</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.3 is about “interpreting and writing manuals, instructions, guidelines” for readers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Its example: a metro ride from Indira Gandhi International (IGI) Airport, New Delhi, to the Noida City Centre metro station in Noida, Uttar Pradesh. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=78s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:18</a>): “Among engineers, scientists, doctors, managers, drivers, GPS, maps and plans have always produced much writing and speaking. Writing/talking in this manner is only slightly different from talking and writing otherwise.” It is not hard, “provided one looks at the map, one looks at the chart carefully and takes all the necessary details” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The four parameters</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Talking/writing here involves much language to do with <strong>sequence, directions, numbers, and options</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=178s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:58</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>It means</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequence</td>\n<td>what comes first, what comes next</td>\n<td>Did the guard blow the whistle before the train started, after it, or at the same time? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Directions</td>\n<td>which way</td>\n<td>north to south or south to north; east to west; in a railway timetable, <strong>up</strong> trains (from the depot to the end of the line) and <strong>down</strong> trains (back to the depot) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Numbers</td>\n<td>exact figures, spelled and said correctly</td>\n<td>Does the train arrive at 5:13 or at 5:30? Is it 13 or 30? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options</td>\n<td>the choices on the way</td>\n<td>enter the station or not; platform 1 or platform 2; for a train driver, the home signal or the outer signal, and when to start braking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Managing these parameters ensures success in complex operations like flying and landing aircrafts, or in performing complex surgeries, landing on Moon and other extra-terrestrial planets and satellites. At the heart of it all, there is language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=178s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:58</a>). Cooking follows the same principle: for a soft, fluffy South Indian idli you must know what to do first and what next, with a choice at each step (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Some mistakes are not forgiven</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You can get away with some mistakes of language”, but not with a mistake in sequence, direction, numbers or options (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). If a student means 13 but spells it “thirtyn”, readers cannot tell whether it is 13 or 30 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). They may pardon a misspelling like “thirtein”, which still points to 13, but not a spelling that points to the wrong number (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). So spell numbers correctly and say them clearly. (A tip of mine: the stress tells them apart when you speak: thir-<strong>TEEN</strong> for 13, <strong>THIR</strong>-ty for 30.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The wrong exit (the lecture’s story at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A senior officer, a woman of his family and a porter (a <em>coolie</em>, who carries the luggage) are late for a train. “Don’t worry,” says the porter; the train stops here for some time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li>They climb the footbridge to reach platform 3. The porter and the officer take the first exit to the left and go down (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The woman walks slowly behind them. She does not see them turn. She walks on to the next exit and cannot find them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li>On the platform the officer looks back for her and cannot find her: panic (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The honest porter finds her, and the train waits long enough. A happy ending, “as it ends in a cinema” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “correct option at the correct point is extremely important” in reading and following any plan, and “the way you write, the way you speak, must be clear enough” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four words for every instruction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Written or spoken, an instruction “should be very clear, should be <strong>unambiguous, precise and brief</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). (<em>Unambiguous</em> = with only one possible meaning. <em>Precise</em> = exact.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-metro-task\">The metro task</h3>\n<p>The activity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=866s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:26</a>): “Look at the attached map, and in about 150 words instruct a friend coming from another country how she/he can reach Noida City Centre Metro Rail station where you can receive him/her. Words of address will not be counted among the total no. of words.”</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What counts in the word limit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many tests do not count the addresses. The count runs from the salutation (“Dear …”) to the closing (“Yours sincerely”) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>First read the map carefully (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=966s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:06</a>). The Delhi Metro has lines named by colour: yellow, green, orange, blue and others. The route (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Leg</th>\n<th>Line</th>\n<th>From → to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Orange</td>\n<td>IGI Airport → NDLS (New Delhi Railway Station)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Yellow</td>\n<td>NDLS → Rajiv Chowk, the next station, “two or three minutes”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Blue</td>\n<td>Rajiv Chowk → Noida City Centre</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Even a child can, following this map, travel comfortably and correctly. So writing should not be a problem. But writing does become a problem” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-draft-and-the-comments\">The first draft and the comments</h3>\n<p>The first draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1098s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:18</a>) is a letter from Jennifer Lee in Noida to her friend Beatrice D’Souza in Manila. It has no subject line (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). It opens (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1116s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:36</a>): “I cannot tell how eagerly I am waiting for your visit. It is ages since I was visited by a friend. But I am sorry much as I wish to I cannot come to the New Delhi airport to receive you, I am so awfully preoccupied until late in the evening that day.”</p>\n<p>The directions are one long paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>): “Of course you must by [buy] the ticket before you board. They will also give you a token using which you can enter the platform, and then take the train to Rajiv Chowk where you can change for the train to Noida City Centre. It is about 10 or 11 stations from there ... So where everyone gets out you can also come out.” It ends: “To welcome you in India, we will have our first dinner at my home. I will cook a great vegetable for you.” <strong>274 words.</strong></p>\n<p>The comments slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1178s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:38</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1374s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:54</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Comment</th>\n<th>In the first draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Unnecessary details</strong></td>\n<td>how happy she is, what she will cook, why she cannot come to the airport (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Vague</strong></td>\n<td>“Do you get the token with the ticket, or do you not? What do you do with it? How long do you hold the same?” If you throw the token away, “you will never be able to come out of the metro rail system” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Unclear</strong></td>\n<td>no time or distance from IGI to Rajiv Chowk, or from there to Noida City Centre; no word that you must <strong>change trains at NDLS</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Unclear</strong></td>\n<td>“How long is it from Rajiv Chowk to Noida City Centre, and does the NC Centre have only one exit gate?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Verbose</strong></td>\n<td>“Has 124 words more than permitted”: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">274</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">124</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One letter, one job</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Send the dinner plans in another mail. “This manual of instruction is not the best possible place” for them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-final-draft-numbered-steps\">The final draft: numbered steps</h3>\n<p>The final draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1456s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:16</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1464s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:24</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>) adds a subject line, “Reg. Your visit to Noida”, and numbers its steps. <strong>153 words.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>The final draft</th>\n<th>What it fixes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Opening</td>\n<td>“Please, see the map attached here. In about an hour you can reach Noida City Centre.”</td>\n<td>the total time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td>“As you come out of the airport, walk to the Metro Rail counter.”</td>\n<td>where to begin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>“Buy a one way ticket to NOIDA CITY CENTRE.”</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>“Retain the ticket and the token until the end of the journey.”</td>\n<td>what to do with the token (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>“Using the token, enter the platform, and take the Orange Line train to NDLS (New Delhi Junction Station). In about 20 minutes, you will reach NDLS. Do not come out of the railway station. On the other side of the platform, take the yellow line train to Huda City Centre. In three minutes, you will reach the next station, Rajiv Chowk.”</td>\n<td>line colours, times, the change at NDLS (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>“Do not go out of the station.”</td>\n<td>keep the token (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>“Change to Blue Line for Noida City Centre.”</td>\n<td>the second change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>“Last station is Noida City Centre.”</td>\n<td>where to get off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>“Take the Exit 1. I will be there.”</td>\n<td>the exact exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>“Happy journey.”</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write the steps in the order they happen</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A common mistake is to write “you will find your train on this platform”, and only then “use this token to enter the platform”. Do you board the train first and enter the platform later? “The sequence of writing must follow the possible sequence of the way a passenger would board a train” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Left or right? Whose left?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Take the exit one, not left or right; that may be confusing: who’s left, who’s right?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>) A name or a number (Exit 1, Platform 2) does not depend on where the reader stands.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Even the final draft can improve</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Step 3 could also say: keep the token carefully, because “you cannot come out of the metro rail system without the token” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>). The test of a good instruction: does it follow the sequence? Does it give distances and directions? Does it say how to come out? “If yes, then it’s a good piece of writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A second model: Mylapore to the Open Air Theatre (on screen at the end of the video, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The last activity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1684s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:04</a>): in about 120 words, instruct a friend coming from Mylapore to the Open Air Theatre (OAT), IIT Madras. A model answer flashes on the screen at the very end, without comment. After the addresses and “Reg. Your visit to Open Air Theatre (OAT)”, it says: “In about 15 minutes you can reach Open Air Theatre (OAT).” Then:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“Buy a one way ticket to Kasturibai Nagar Railway Station.”</li>\n<li>“Take the first exit at Kasturibai Nagar.”</li>\n<li>“Cross the main road and walk for about 500 metres towards Anna University.”</li>\n<li>“At your left, you can see the main gate of IIT Madras.”</li>\n<li>“Collect the entry token from the security guard on your left.”</li>\n<li>“Walk a few metres further to the bus stop.”</li>\n<li>“Board the yellow bus and get down at Gajendra circle, which is the fourth stop.”</li>\n<li>“I will be there.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> the same shape. The total time comes first, then one action per step, with names, numbers and the exit. Here “at your left” is safe, because step 3 has already fixed the direction in which the reader walks.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise with any map</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Take any map and write instructions for your friends or family: how to reach your office in this building, or your room in the hostel. “Write it, revise it, write it, revise it” until you are satisfied (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>). We take about five years to learn our first language, though we hear it all the time; a second language takes as long, so read and write a little every day (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>). “Writing about a map or a design or a plan is very easy, provided you read the map carefully before you start and you write each step as they ought to be followed” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"personal-details-in-a-business-letter\">Personal details in a business letter</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.4. A business letter is usually impersonal: it has nothing personal in it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). But the slide says (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=102s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:42</a>): “Even in business writing, sometimes we have to share personal information. For instance, we travel to a new location, we have to tell our host about our stay, food etc.” In a new city we must eat, stay and travel, and we may want to see the sights and buy gifts, so we ask our local partners for help (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). After about 10 hours of work, 2 hours of preparing and 7 or 8 hours of sleep, a visitor still has “about 3 or 4 hours” free (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The next slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=142s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:22</a>): “But a lot of people just casually mention some sightseeing preferences. It is important to mention even these personal things precisely and clearly. Many tests of proficiency in English have parts of this kind of writing in their tests.”</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask in advance</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Mention your needs “well in time, well in advance of your visit”, so that your partner can plan and keep time free for you (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why in advance? Two hazards (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Your attention splits.</strong> In the meeting you keep thinking, “When shall I ask him what to see here?”, and you cannot give full attention to the business in hand.</li>\n<li><strong>You spring a surprise.</strong> “Can you come with me at 5 o’clock this evening?” Your partner has a family, other clients, other plans. Either she says no, which is hard for both of you, or she says yes unwillingly: you feel guilty, and she is irritated all along.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Business-like, formal and polite</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>State your preferences “in a business-like manner”, “not being romantic about it or being apologetic about it or being vague about it”. “It is possible to be formal and polite ... there is no conflict between these” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">What the examiner checks</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Tests such as IELTS and TOEFL include tasks like this (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). Grammar, vocabulary, spelling and punctuation are the lower-level skills. Here the examiner wants to see whether you can <strong>plan</strong> an answer, and whether you can tell the <strong>trivial</strong> (unimportant) from the important, the insignificant from the significant (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-task-a-trip-to-blackpool\">The task: a trip to Blackpool</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:50</a>): “In about 150 words, write a letter to a business partner abroad giving him the details of your forthcoming travel to meet him. You can mention your personal preferences in room, food, etc during your stay there. You can also say if you would wish to see some tourist spots in that city and nearby.”</p>\n<p>The lecture’s setting: you export goods, and you must visit a small European city to renegotiate your rates and delivery schedule and to answer complaints about quality, before you lose the client (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). The city is <strong>Blackpool</strong> in the UK, near the Lake District (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=642s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:42</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Remember the rules of business writing: 150 words, a fixed format, and “more important things first” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rough-draft-243-words\">The rough draft: 243 words</h3>\n<p>The rough draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=738s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:18</a>) is from Maya Lobo Prabhu of Konkan Exports, Mangalore, to Mr Peter P Sumner in Blackpool, with the subject “A Business Trip”. The lecturer calls this kind of letter “sloppy, casual, vague, more words than necessary”, and warns that a lot of people really write like this (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The rough draft says</th>\n<th>The problem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“You will be happy to know that I am eagerly looking forward to my visit to your great country. I have heard so much about your country.”</td>\n<td>What does it mean? What have you heard? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“I have already booked my flight ticket, business class”</td>\n<td>not needed; it may look like <strong>showing off</strong> (boasting), which is “in bad taste” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“I am reaching Heathrow London at three a m on 17 March”</td>\n<td>No one is coming to receive you there, so the hour does not matter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“In London I may visit an Indian friend for a few hours.”</td>\n<td>your own business (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Please tell me the name of the hotel you are going to book for me. It will be a joy to see you at the railway station, but why take unnecessary trouble.”</td>\n<td>When do you reach his city? Do you want him at the station or not? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“I will take a taxi and go to the hotel directly from the railway station.”</td>\n<td>of course: “there are no auto-rickshaws in Blackpool, no horses, no bullock carts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“I hope you have good hotel in your city.”</td>\n<td>What is “good”? Unless you define it, accept what your partner finds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Kindly tell them that we in India take only vegetarian food, so I would like to have good vegetables and fruits and milk with rice and sambhar.”</td>\n<td>Hotels know what Indians eat, and not all Indians are vegetarian; your partner is not going to cook for you. Just ask for vegetarian food (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Are there good tourist places in your city?”</td>\n<td>You can check that on Google (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Since my husband is not coming with me, I should bring some gift for him.”</td>\n<td>it can easily be misread (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Can you please advise me which shops I should go to so that I get good discounts and good quality materials at a very cheap rate?”</td>\n<td>Shop names are not needed now. And “good discount” is vague: 2 rupees off 98, or 98 off 100? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The comments slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:50</a>): “The first draft has 100 words more than the limit for this letter. It has many items of unnecessary information. In vital details, it is vague. Inaccurate and redundant words can confuse the reader.” (It has 243 words: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">243</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">93</span></span></span></span>,</span> almost 100 too many.) What it should say, it does not: “when you are reaching, where you are reaching, what you will eat, where would you like to go and see, what kind of shopping” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). The verdict: “redundant, confused, vague, all the three” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-final-draft-152-words\">The final draft: 152 words</h3>\n<p>The subject line becomes “Forthcoming trip to the U K” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1342s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:22</a>). The body (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1358s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:38</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I will reach Blackpool by the 4:30 train from London on 17 March. I can take a taxi from there to the hotel, and can call you after checking in. We can meet the following morning in your office.</p>\n<p>Please, help me get a room with a window. Vegetarian food, I believe, is not a problem in the U K. And some tea and ordinary water should be enough for drinks for me.</p>\n<p>Time permitting, I may join a conducted tour of the Lake District. But can I, please, invite Mrs Sumner and you to join me for a walking tour of a nearby village one afternoon, and then we may have some quiet dinner in a local restaurant. With Mrs Sumner’s help I may also buy for my husband some memento from a local shop.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It closes “Looking forward to see you, Yours sincerely, Maria”. (<em>Memento</em> = a small thing you keep to remember a place.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The first sentence does the most work (the lecture’s analysis at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“I will reach Blackpool by the 4:30 train from London on 17 March.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Where:</strong> Blackpool, the partner’s own city, not Heathrow or London. “No matter whatever else you had done before”, what matters is when you reach your destination (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>How:</strong> by train, not by taxi, boat or plane (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Which train:</strong> the 4:30 train from London.</li>\n<li><strong>Which day:</strong> 17 March.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“In one sentence, you have told your reader when to expect you and how to expect you” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). The next sentences free the partner from coming to the station: you will take a taxi, call after checking in, and meet him the next morning in his office (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>How the final draft says it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrival</td>\n<td>where, how, when and which day, in the first sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Room</td>\n<td>“a room with a window”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Food and drink</td>\n<td>vegetarian food; tea and ordinary water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sightseeing</td>\n<td>a conducted tour of the Lake District, “time permitting”; a walk to a nearby village and dinner, with Mr and Mrs Sumner invited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shopping</td>\n<td>a memento for her husband from a local shop</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Personal details too must be given “with the greatest possible clarity, and in a rank-ordered manner”: important things first, less important next, and things of little importance at the end “if word limit permits; if it does not, forget it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Looking forward to see you”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The final draft ends “Looking forward to see you”. In standard English the <em>to</em> in <em>look forward to</em> is a preposition, so the verb after it takes <em>-ing</em>: <strong>Looking forward to seeing you.</strong> (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">More tasks of this kind</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The activity slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1498s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:58</a>): in about 150 words, write a mail or a letter to (1) your host, with whom you will stay for six weeks during an internship in Edinburgh; (2) a friend who will receive you at an airport in New York; (3) a librarian at the British Library, London, asking her to help you find paying-guest accommodation nearby for a three-month research fellowship. All of them follow the same principles: a strict format, and the most important things first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"data-interpretation-from-visual-to-verbal\">Data interpretation: from visual to verbal</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.5. Many reports turn numbers into words: “from visual to verbal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Why use numbers at all? Because they are <strong>objective</strong> (based on facts anyone can check, not on feelings). “I live in a very comfortable place”: what is comfortable? “Though this is the month of December, I live at a place with 22 degrees temperature”: now you need not say <em>comfortable</em>; everyone understands (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=68s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:08</a>): “Those in engineering, management and other disciplines frequently write about statistical data. These data may come in the form of bar charts, histograms, graphs, pie charts, and other kinds of statistical tables.” In engineering, management, the sciences, pharmacy and biotechnology, statistical data are “the spine of the report”, “the vertebral column of the report” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The forms of data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Table:</strong> numbers in rows and columns.</li>\n<li><strong>Bar chart:</strong> bars whose lengths show amounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Histogram:</strong> bars side by side, one for each range of values, showing how many values fall in each range.</li>\n<li><strong>Graph:</strong> often a line that shows a change, for example over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Pie chart:</strong> a circle cut into slices; each slice is a share of the whole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The short meanings are mine. How these charts are drawn is in <a href=\"#/stats1/w02\">Statistics 1, Week 2</a>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two charts from the slides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>“The Indian automotive industry: growth and opportunities”</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=204s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:24</a>): “Light Vehicles have grown from 0.7m, in 2001, to 3.5m in 2011”, with a chart of turnover and growth from 2007-08 to 2020-21. The industry was small before 2007, and has grown fast since foreign companies began to manufacture in India (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). Statistical data “give you evidence for whatever claim you may be making” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Coronavirus: India’s Covid-19 cases”</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:40</a>): a graph of the cases crossing one million.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Visual to verbal</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A chart turns numbers into a picture. A report turns the picture back into words: “The information that is there in the form of numbers, and words, changes into a running text” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=326s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:26</a>). (<em>Running text</em> = connected sentences.) A good report is “not just a verbatim translation” (word-for-word copy) of what you see; it also takes in “things that you do not generally see” in the chart (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-data-report-must-say\">What a data report must say</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The six points of a data report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“While writing a report on this sort of data, we must pay attention to the following” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=334s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:34</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(a) What it is all about.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>(b) How it is presented statistically:</strong> a bar chart, a graph, a histogram, a pie chart?</li>\n<li><strong>(c) Main trends in the data:</strong> what “hits the viewer in one glance”.</li>\n<li><strong>(d) Significant details:</strong> “details that cannot be ignored”.</li>\n<li><strong>(e) Some differences between different nodes:</strong> “between two periods it grew slowly, but between two other periods it grew pretty rapidly, maybe twice as fast or three times as fast”.</li>\n<li><strong>(f) Any concluding remarks.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>) “You could do more, but don’t do less” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trend and node</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>trend</strong> is the general direction of the numbers: rising, falling or steady.</li>\n<li>A <strong>node</strong> is one point in the data, such as one year or one group. The difference between two nodes, or between two stretches of time, is often where the story is.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The word band</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>These reports “are written in about 150 words, especially at English proficiency tests” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=444s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:24</a>). Five words more or less (145 or 155) is not penalised. But 130 or 170 loses a percentage of the marks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). Academic writing is “brief, precise, focused, to the point”, and in simple language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-facebook-table\">The Facebook table</h3>\n<p>“Facebook users in January 2018” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=566s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:26</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind of users</th>\n<th>No. of users</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Monthly Active Users</td>\n<td>2.13 B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily Active Users</td>\n<td>1.40 B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DAU in Asia</td>\n<td>499 M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Average Friends for Males</td>\n<td>145 M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Average Friends for Females</td>\n<td>166 M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily Video Views</td>\n<td>100 M hrs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time spent per user per day</td>\n<td>20 Mins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total World Population</td>\n<td>7.6 B</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(B = billion, M = million, DAU = daily active users.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First draft, 213 words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=642s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:42</a>)</th>\n<th>Final draft, 149 words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=668s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:08</a>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“The table presents statistical data about number of Facebook users and the frequency of their use. It is represented in the form of a table. There are two columns and 8 rows.”</td>\n<td>“The table shows the number of Facebook users and the frequency of their use. The data are for January, 2018.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Of the total number of global users of Facebook, as reported above, a little more than 50% are active daily users.”</td>\n<td>“A little over 50% of them are active daily users.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Interestingly, both men and women have lots of ‘friends’ but, with about 166 million friends for women, they exceed the number of friends of friends for men by over 10%.”</td>\n<td>“All users have ‘friends’ but, with about 166 million, women exceed men by over 10% in the number of friends.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“It also indicates that a good lot of people, allegedly young, seem to be nearly always there. As media pandits have always believed, the world is fast turning into a global village.”</td>\n<td>“Many people, allegedly young, seem to be always there. World is fast turning into a global village.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What changed:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The period is given:</strong> “The data are for January, 2018.” Without the period, “your report doesn’t mean anything” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>No obvious facts:</strong> not “it is represented in the form of a table” (the reader can see that), not “as reported above”.</li>\n<li><strong>Trends, not a copy:</strong> “you do not produce the numbers mechanically; you get the trends, you get significant details” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Fewer words</strong> for the same facts.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Turning numbers into insight (the lecture’s reasoning at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Chunks make numbers easy to use: “if you say 40% of 1 billion, then that means you are talking about 400 million people” <span class=\"nw\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span></span></span></span></span> million <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span></span></span></span></span> million) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A manager can act on that: “if I advertise in this medium, 400 million people are likely to see my ad” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The gender split: on average women have more friends than men: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">166</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">145</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span></span></span></span></span> million, “maybe 20, 21 million” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). That is small next to a billion, but it shows a <strong>trend</strong>: more and more women are joining social media and spending longer there, so a business can reach them this way (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The concluding remark: the world is “rapidly becoming one big community”, a <strong>global village</strong>, and the data are “a window on our changing world” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> good reports “also look inside the data, and they share those insights” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Check the numbers before you copy a model (my arithmetic)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The final draft says</th>\n<th>What the table gives</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“A little over 50% of them are active daily users”</td>\n<td><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.13</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.66</span></span></span></span>:</span></span> about two-thirds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Asia accounts for nearly 40% of the daily active users”</td>\n<td><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">499</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.36</span></span></span></span>:</span></span> about 36%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Of the 2.13 billion global users, nearly 50% are in Asia”</td>\n<td>the table gives only Asia’s <strong>daily</strong> users: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">499</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">130</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.23</span></span></span></span> of all users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“women exceed men by over 10%”</td>\n<td><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">145</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.145</span></span></span></span>:</span></span> about 14.5% more, so “over 10%” is right</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>As Lecture 11.6 says, “one needs to look at the chart carefully”: every number in a report must come from the data.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The activity slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:10</a>) gives a table, “Doctors in India”, with the number of doctors in six states and in all India in 2000 and in 2005. Look at any such table “for about five minutes”, find what is significant, write about 150 words, and revise “until you feel that you can bring no change anymore” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"a-data-report-the-household-survey\">A data report: the household survey</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.6. “No chart, no graph is complete by itself without report, without comments on the numbers”: why the numbers are what they are (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=126s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:06</a>): “Writing a report on data from Pie Charts, Histograms, or graphs is not very different from doing so for data in a table.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The five focus points</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The report should focus on: a. What these data are about; b. How they are presented; c. Important Highlights; d. Significant Details/ Noteworthy Inter-nodal differences; e. Concluding Remarks” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=152s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:32</a>).</p>\n<p>These are the six points of Lecture 11.5, with “significant details” and “differences between nodes” joined into one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point</th>\n<th>The lecturer explains</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What the data are about</td>\n<td>an <strong>overarching statement</strong> (one that covers everything): “Do they describe buffaloes or cows? ... lions or tigers? Are they about climate?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How they are presented</td>\n<td>a pie chart or a bar diagram? How many sections? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Important highlights</td>\n<td>the most important points that come out of the chart (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Significant details</td>\n<td>details that change the picture, and the differences between nodes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concluding remarks</td>\n<td>Do the facts lead to one conclusion? If yes, say it. If not, say that “nothing can be formally concluded”; that is still effective (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The railway timetable (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A timetable has columns for the train’s number and name, the days it runs, and its arrival and departure times (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li>You look up one train, say 19263, the Porbandar–Delhi Sarai express (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The <strong>overall highlight</strong>: “this chart gives us the names of trains, frequency or the days on which they run, and their arrival and departure time” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>significant detail</strong>: “in all there are ten trains to your city, though only 2 of them begin here” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A difference between nodes (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A business grew fast between 2010 and 2015, but not between 2015 and 2020.</li>\n<li>Ask why. The answer: the local government had policies that did not help the business.</li>\n<li>A chart only shows the numbers. A report can show the difference between the two stretches, and its cause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-task-and-the-chart\">The task and the chart</h3>\n<p>“In about 150 words, write a report on the basis of data presented in the following pie chart for a household survey conducted recently” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). The chart gives no date and no place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Slice of the pie</th>\n<th>Share of respondents</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Housewife/unemployed</td>\n<td>56%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Domestic help</td>\n<td>28%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tradesperson</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other service job</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-employed</td>\n<td>2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labourer</td>\n<td>2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>Respondents</em> = the people who answered the survey. Check: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">56</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">28</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span>.)</span></span></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-draft-copied-numbers-and-a-tangent\">The first draft: copied numbers and a tangent</h3>\n<p>The first draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:50</a>) begins: “The pie chart here presents data of some women respondents of a household survey. Of the total number of respondents 56 % respondents are housewives and unemployed. 28% ladies work as domestic help, 7% have their own shops and business ...” Then it goes on (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=658s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:58</a>): “The chart shows great unemployment among women. Where as all men are employed and get jobs these days, women still do not get jobs. Government should bring reservation for women in all departments ...” <strong>146 words.</strong></p>\n<p>The comments slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=820s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:40</a>): “The draft given above meets the requirement of word limit almost correctly. It goes on a tangent talking about denial of employment to women. But other details are left unmentioned. Data are just mechanically reproduced.”</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Mechanical reproduction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing out the chart’s numbers one by one: “x is x, y is y” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). The pie chart already shows them, so “you get no wiser” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tangent</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A sudden move away from the subject to something else. Here the draft jumps to women’s rights, reservation and equality. The lecturer agrees that there must be justice and equality for all, “but is this the place to say all this?” It is “gas, anger waiting to come out, but not information that can make you wiser” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inside the word limit is not enough</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>146 words is fine for a 150-word task. “But what will attract penalty here is the absence of information, the absence of insight” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Every test item has a purpose. Here you must show your language <strong>and</strong> your “ability to analyse, evaluate and make predictions on the basis of data” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-final-draft-insight-not-a-copy\">The final draft: insight, not a copy</h3>\n<p>Its title: “Women’s employment: a household survey” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:20</a>). <strong>152 words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:50</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The final draft</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“The given pie-chart shows how respondents are engaged by way of work.”</td>\n<td>(a) what the data are about (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“The chart has six sections, one each for a kind of work.”</td>\n<td>(b) how they are presented (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Chart does not mention absolute number of respondents, all women, or time of survey.”</td>\n<td>says what the chart does <strong>not</strong> tell (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Section 1 shows that over half the women are housewives or unemployed.”</td>\n<td>(c) the main highlight, as a chunk: 56% → “over half” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“It is unclear if they are all unemployed even when they have tried for a job.”</td>\n<td>questions the label (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Most women work as domestic help in other houses. One fourth of all respondents are employed this way.”</td>\n<td>(d) a significant detail: 28%, a little more than a quarter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Another nine per cent are self-employed, at least two percent of them being in trade in some manner.”</td>\n<td>tradesperson 7% + self-employed 2% = 9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Of the total only two percent respondents have said they work as labour, but five percent have claimed they are employed in services other than domestic help or manual labour.”</td>\n<td>the small slices (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“The chart shows that new sectors of employment are yet to open for women.”</td>\n<td>(e) the concluding remark (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Chunks</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Round, easy shares said in words: “over half”, “one fourth”, “a little more than one-fourth”. Saying “more than half the women” has a “greater impact” than “56 per cent” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). But stay accurate: 28% is “a little more than one-fourth ... not 25 per cent” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A misleading label</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The chart puts housewives and the unemployed in one slice. Strictly, housewives are not unemployed: they do a 24×7 job that is not recognised. So “it is unclear if they are all unemployed or only some”, and how many of them tried to find a job. “To that extent that section of the data is misleading” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). A good report says so in one calm sentence, without a speech.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fewer words for the same fact</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1188s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:48</a>): “One needs to look at the chart carefully. One can write ‘over’ in place of ‘more than’, or ‘under’ in place of ‘less than’ etc.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Long</th>\n<th>Short</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>more than so many</td>\n<td>over …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>less than so many</td>\n<td>under …, fewer …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>of the total number of respondents, …</td>\n<td>all respondents …</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Biased definitions cost words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The first draft says the self-employed are those who “work in their own homes or companies”. But the self-employed do not always work at home: a woman who runs a school is self-employed, and she is not at home all day. “These are prejudices, biases; they make for bad writing, and they take a lot of words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">More than language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both drafts are in good English. “A good piece of writing requires more than language: it requires a sharp eye, a sharp mind, for details”, to decide what is relevant and how much to say (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Look at the chart until you have all its information in your mind. Write the first draft “without a thought for language”, then ask: what is necessary? What can be left unsaid? What can be said in fewer words? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>) When you have really learned something, “you will notice your mistakes as soon as you make them” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The activity slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1426s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:46</a>) is a chart, “Who are India’s farmers?” (from the Agricultural Census 2015-16): 86.2% marginal and small farms (0–2 hectares), 13.2% semi-medium and medium farms (2–10 hectares), 0.6% large farms (10 hectares and above). Write about it in the same way. Once a week, take some numbers from a newspaper, social media or a website, and write a short report (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lldu_p3qVcI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-an-essay\">Writing an essay</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.7. Every school, college and university teaches essay writing, and it is “considered to be the ultimate test” of a language: it tests all of your English except speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Real life needs it too: engineers, doctors and project managers write long pieces, and newspapers print essays and features (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What an essay tests</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Language:</strong> “Grammatically correct language, good writing skill, knowledge of spelling, vocabulary, punctuation and other conventions of writing are all important” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=202s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:22</a>); a variety of sentences and words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge of the world</strong>, not only of the language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Hierarchy</strong> (an order of ranks): can you arrange ideas, “more importantly, from important to unimportant”? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Content</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“What do you write, and how much do you write?” Do you have arguments, examples, data and evidence in support, “or is it just your prejudice?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>) The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=258s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:18</a>): “Contents for an essay can come mainly from general knowledge, they can be supplemented from other sources, at the internet, and with experts.” Like a newspaper reporter, gather the relevant knowledge first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). You need not be an expert, but you need “a reasonably good knowledge of the given topic” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Planning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A good essay needs planning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=258s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:18</a>). In about 250 words you must know what to mention first and what next, and “that link, that chain must continue” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Examples and evidence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A good essay reads well if it has good examples, has evidence and arguments based upon good ideas” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=258s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:18</a>). Examples also keep the reader reading: a well-written essay can be as enjoyable as plays and poems (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-task-agriculture-in-india\">The task: agriculture in India</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=424s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:04</a>): “In about 250 words, write an essay on Agriculture in India. Synopsis: In your essay, you can include the following: its contribution to the India economy, history of agriculture in India, unique features of Indian agriculture – its present problems and privileges, - its future.”</p>\n<p>The lecturer’s questions for each point (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point</th>\n<th>Ask</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Contribution to the economy</td>\n<td>What has agriculture given to the Indian economy?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History</td>\n<td>Has it ever been profitable, or enjoyable, to be a farmer?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unique features</td>\n<td>It used few machines, and animal power instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present problems and privileges</td>\n<td>What are they now?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future</td>\n<td>Will agriculture survive in India?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-draft-594-words\">The first draft: 594 words</h3>\n<p>The writer of the first draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=544s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:04</a>) hardly makes a mistake of grammar, punctuation or vocabulary, but “has not been able to take them together”: there are “lots of redundancies, lots of trivialities, lots of inaccuracies” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). It has <strong>594 words</strong> for a task of about 250, so “the very first condition” is broken (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>): <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">594</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">250</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">344</span></span></span></span></span> words too many.</p>\n<p>It opens: “Just now in India farmers are in the news headlines. Suddenly they have hundreds of millions of sympathisers ...” That claim is “blatantly false”: farmers are always in the news (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Irrelevant ideas” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=596s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:56</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What is</th>\n<th>What should be</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the entire first paragraph</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd paragraph: 158 words for one idea, “agriculture used to be important”</td>\n<td>“Five words would be enough: Agriculture used to be important.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100 words for saying it has earned foreign exchange for India</td>\n<td>“This point can be made in a few words.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>agriculture in independent India: little or no data</td>\n<td>“Should be stated with some data.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>making agriculture a popular career: hardly any example to show why new policies can succeed</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>but no, or nearly no, error of grammar or spelling</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>These are “the products of a confused head”: there are no figures on crops or farmers, and no example for a claim such as “making agriculture a popular career” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"vague-words-cliche-s-and-jargon\">Vague words, clichés and jargon</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Cliché, jargon, vague word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>cliché</strong> is an expression used so often that it is “worn out” and no longer means anything: “the pathetic state of agriculture in India” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). The lecturer also calls such ready-made phrases <strong>jargon</strong>: “thoughtless expressions”.</li>\n<li>A <strong>vague</strong> word gives no clear measure. “A tremendous body of knowledge”: “is it five square kilometres or is it one teaspoonful?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Vague words, cliché, jargon” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=798s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:18</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1036s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:16</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What is</th>\n<th>What should be, and why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“hundreds of millions of sympathisers...”</td>\n<td>“Numerous / Innumerable...”: why exaggerate? One word does the job (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“freedom to sell where they wish to might bring them better prices”</td>\n<td>“freedom to sell where they wish to might bring farmers better prices...”: too many <em>they</em> and <em>them</em>, so repeat the noun (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...the pathetic state of agriculture in India”</td>\n<td>cut: a cliché (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...is more or less based on roles and responsibilities...”</td>\n<td>“is based mostly on roles ...”: <em>more or less</em> becomes <em>mostly</em>; and a role brings its responsibility, so one word is enough (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...a tremendous body of knowledge...”</td>\n<td>“...a body of knowledge...”: <em>tremendous</em> is vague (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...abandoning their centuries old farms and farm lands...”</td>\n<td>“...abandoning their farms and farm lands...”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...modernizing and developing agricultural technology, though lots of agriculture universities were opened in India. Crop insurance is nowhere visible except on paper.”</td>\n<td>“...modernizing agricultural technology, though many agriculture universities were opened. Crop insurance is yet to be available.”: shorter and plainer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...corruption being so endemic in government offices...”</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“...agriculture is no longer the thing that attracts anyone but one without a choice...”</td>\n<td>“...agriculture attracts only the unemployable.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“..., just as other products in other sectors are allowed to. Once again agriculture can regain its place of pride, and can enrich all associated with it”</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“In all of these cases ... errors have occurred not because the writer does not know English; errors occur because writers have not applied their mind; they have written mechanically” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pronouns that pile up</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Freedom to sell where they wish to might bring them better prices.” After many words, <em>they</em> and <em>them</em> could mean the farmers, the sympathisers or the government. “Three or four pronouns together can become quite confusing.” So repeat the noun at least once: “...might bring <strong>farmers</strong> better prices” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). “Writing vague, unclear sentences is the worst enemy of a good piece of writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-final-draft-252-words\">The final draft: 252 words</h3>\n<p>The final draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1074s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:54</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1136s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:56</a>) has four paragraphs. The lecturer sums up its plan: agriculture’s importance in the economy, the liability it has become, the tools it uses, the policies that have been its undoing, and the policies that will make it better (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paragraph</th>\n<th>What it says</th>\n<th>Synopsis point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>“Agriculture is the oldest industry in India. Until recently, it provided employment to over 80% of India’s population. It also brought most of India’s foreign exchange. India has everything for good agriculture, soil, water, sunshine, and a culture.”</td>\n<td>contribution, history, unique features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>“But agriculture no longer has the old importance. It is not a profitable occupation any more.” Plots made uneconomical for large-scale production; no investment in modern technology; crop insurance “only upon paper”; farmers not “paid fairly and promptly”</td>\n<td>present problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Wrong policies on finance, fertilisers and pesticides, the neglect of inherited methods and the lack of marketing “have reduced agriculture from a profit generating industry into a subsidy-dependent industry. This must change.”</td>\n<td>present problems, and their cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>“New policies must be introduced for financing agriculture, for marketing of its produce ...” It ends: “With some determined investment and industrial freedom, agriculture can regain its place of pride, and can enrich all.”</td>\n<td>the future</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The essay now covers “its present problems”, “its past glory” and “how it can be rescued from sick bed”, and it does so “because of planning and revision” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Plan first, then revise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Take time to start writing an essay, but think of all the points you would like to include, and then order them in a certain manner” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>). No draft is final (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Keep a <strong>dictionary</strong> and a <strong>thesaurus</strong> (a book of words with similar meanings) near you, and have “the patience to revise and rewrite” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Then let friends, family or reviewers comment: “excellence lies only in constant pursuit” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Topics to practise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The activity slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1208s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:08</a>): about 250 words on each of these: the textile industry of India; local foods in global times; the future of local languages; traditional Indian marriage; Indian cinema; Indian sports. The lecturer adds more: big malls or street-corner shops? The future of Indian dress. Should we close our kitchens at home and order all meals from outside? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>) These lists are “indicative, not exhaustive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). If you can write good essays, your listening, reading and speaking improve too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-qpN5ilfDy8&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-power-of-simplicity\">The power of simplicity</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 11.8. All the writing sessions said the same thing: write simple sentences and use simple, everyday, common words. Then “you will be understood best, you will hardly make mistakes”, and you and your readers will enjoy it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=122s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:02</a>): “Simplicity works best everywhere. This is true also of languages. Best books and talks have happened in simple words &amp; sentences.” Simple language “is understood best, is read and heard by most people, remembered by most people, and therefore the writing survives longer; it goes far” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>What the lecture says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Buddha</strong></td>\n<td>Sanskrit was the language of scholars in his time, but he taught in simple language and advised his followers to speak and write simply; “that is why in no time he became popular” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shakespeare</strong></td>\n<td>only a grammar-school education (some Latin grammar and a little arithmetic), no university; yet popular in his own time and still today. His audience was mostly working people, not scholars (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wordsworth</strong></td>\n<td>wrote “about common things in common language that could be understood by all”, and became Poet Laureate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dickens</strong></td>\n<td>“wrote about the joys and sorrows of the poor, the weak and the meek, and made ‘Novel’ a popular literary form” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=826s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:46</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hawking</strong></td>\n<td><em>A Brief History of Time</em>, a book on astrophysics, in simple sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1116s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:36</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"shakespeare-the-opening-of-macbeth\">Shakespeare: the opening of <em>Macbeth</em></h3>\n<p>From <em>Macbeth</em>, Act 1, Scene 1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=318s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:18</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=508s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:28</a>). Three witches meet in a desert place, in thunder and lightning.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Speaker</th>\n<th>Line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First Witch</td>\n<td>When shall we three meet again / In thunder, lightning, or in rain?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second Witch</td>\n<td>When the hurlyburly’s done, / When the battle’s lost and won.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third Witch</td>\n<td>That will be ere the set of sun.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First Witch</td>\n<td>Where the place?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second Witch</td>\n<td>Upon the heath.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third Witch</td>\n<td>There to meet with Macbeth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All</td>\n<td>Fair is foul, and foul is fair: / Hover through the fog and filthy air.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>“Can you imagine a plainer question?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Hurlyburly</em> = noise and confusion; perhaps the one hard word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Ere</em> = before. The word has gone out of use, but it was common in Shakespeare’s time: “ere the set of sun” = before sunset (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Heath</em> = open wild land where few people live (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“Fair is foul, and foul is fair” is “a bit complex, a bit metaphysical”, but enough to catch the audience’s attention (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:20</a>): “All are simple sentences, and nearly all words are of one syllable. Many other writers and speakers in all ages wrote in this manner.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"wordsworth-composed-upon-westminster-bridge\">Wordsworth: “Composed upon Westminster Bridge”</h3>\n<p>When the Industrial Revolution began, the skies of cities were dark with smoke. One morning Wordsworth stood on a bridge over the river Thames in London and found the sky surprisingly clear, with the sun rising (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). The poem begins (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=624s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:24</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Earth has not anything to show more fair:<br>\nDull would he be of soul who could pass by<br>\nA sight so touching in its majesty:<br>\nThis City now doth, like a garment, wear<br>\nThe beauty of the morning; silent, bare,<br>\nShips, towers, domes, theatres, and temples lie<br>\nOpen unto the fields, and to the sky;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>“Earth has not anything to show more fair” means: this is the most beautiful sight on earth (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). <em>Ships, towers, domes, theatres</em>: words that everyone in that community knows (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:50</a>): “Except perhaps for words like ‘splendour’, ‘glittering’ and ‘glideth’, nearly all other words in this poem by a poet laureate of England are monosyllabic words of common use.” (<em>Monosyllabic</em> = of one syllable. <em>Glideth</em> is an old form of <em>glides</em>. A <em>poet laureate</em> is the official poet of the king or queen (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).)</p>\n<h3 id=\"dickens-and-hawking\">Dickens and Hawking</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Oliver Twist</em> (the lecture’s passage at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=928s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:28</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>&quot;The surgeon had been sitting with his face turned towards the fire: giving the palms of his hands a warm and a rub alternately. As the young woman spoke, he rose, and advancing to the bed’s head, said, with more kindness than might have been expected of him: ‘Oh, you must not talk about dying yet.’</p>\n<p>‘Lor bless her dear heart, no!’ interposed the nurse, hastily depositing in her pocket a green glass bottle, the contents of which she had been tasting in a corner with evident satisfaction.&quot;</p>\n<p>The scene is the birth of Oliver in a <em>workhouse</em> (a place where poor people were made to work long hours in hard conditions); his mother dies soon after (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). The surgeon warms his own hands instead of caring for the patient; the nurse drinks secretly from her bottle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Yet “the sentence, the words, they’re all simple, mostly common words of everyday use” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>A Brief History of Time</em> (the lecture’s passage at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1116s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:36</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Our present ideas about the motion of bodies date back to Galileo and Newton. Before them people believed Aristotle, who said that the natural state of a body was to be at rest and that it moved only if driven by a force or impulse. It followed that a heavy body should fall faster than a light one, because it would have a greater pull toward the earth.” (Chapter 2, page 10)</p>\n<p>The first sentence has <strong>one verb</strong>: <em>date back</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). Great writing is simple in science and in business too, not only in literature (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"friends-and-the-public\">Friends and the public</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">In-group jargon</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Among friends, chat words such as <em>LOL</em> (laughing out loud) and <em>ROFL</em> (rolling on the floor laughing) are fine; “your group membership will depend upon jargon” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). The slide shows such messages: “Cool then”, “Wow man!!! Super.”, “Chill bro.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1228s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:28</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write formally for the public</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When you write for the public, your language must be <strong>formal</strong>. Many readers do not know these words, and “it is these people, the older ones, who judge your applications for jobs, for project fundings, for fellowships”. “So it is best to write formal, simple language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-write-simple-sentences\">How to write simple sentences</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The five rules</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “How to write simple sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1558s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:58</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“Do not write sentences with more than 10 words.” Six, seven or eight words; nine or ten at most. If a sentence grows longer, “try and break the sentence into two” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“Write with only one verb.” “One verb means a simple sentence; two verbs means either complex or compound” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“Write only one or two syllable words, unless unavoidable.” Sometimes you cannot help it: <em>possible</em> has three syllables, and so has <em>feasible</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“Do write more than one draft.” With each draft the writing becomes simpler (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“Use a dictionary and thesaurus when in doubt” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A beat of a word, built around one vowel sound: <em>help</em> (1 syllable), <em>bod-y</em> (2), <em>pos-si-ble</em> (3). <a href=\"#/english1/w08/syllables\">Week 8</a> explains syllables in full.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple, compound and complex sentences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple:</strong> one subject with one verb. <em>It saves time.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Compound:</strong> two simple sentences joined by <em>and</em>, <em>but</em> or <em>or</em>. <em>It saves time, and it saves money.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Complex:</strong> a main sentence plus a part that depends on it, joined by a word such as <em>because</em>, <em>when</em> or <em>which</em>. <em>I cook at home because it saves money.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The examples are mine; the lecture gives the rule: one verb, a simple sentence.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Home-cooked food: two drafts (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1728s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:48</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The task: a paragraph of about 150 words on “Home cooked food vs Ready to eat door delivered food” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1514s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:14</a>).</p>\n<p>Draft 1 has <strong>164 words</strong>: too many, and “many examinations penalize you for exceeding words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). Draft 2 has <strong>144 words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1772s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:32</a>). Both open: “Home cooked food is the best. It suits your taste. It saves time and money. It is good for health.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Draft 1</th>\n<th>Draft 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“In a family, it creates bonding.”</td>\n<td>“In a family, it helps bonding.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“We should share home work.”</td>\n<td>“All can share home work.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“They hardly get to sit, work and be together.”</td>\n<td>“They are hardly ever together.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Such spots are occasions of great education for the young.”</td>\n<td>“But such spots can be education for the young.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Not the best universities can compete even with an ordinary family in this aspect of education.”</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“There is however only one problem in cooking at home. We have to spend time buying and storing stuff.”</td>\n<td>“There is however one problem. For cooking at home, we have to spend time buying and storing stuff.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “almost the entire essay has monosyllabic words, simple sentences, sentences with only one verb” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). The gain: “you make no mistakes, everyone understands you, and you enjoy writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). Even in the examination hall you can write more than one draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Binding and bonding</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Binding:</strong> the relationship itself: father and son, brothers, uncle and nephew.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonding:</strong> “the way we feel for one another”. You feel strongly for your mother, so you have a greater bond with her (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Home-cooked food “promotes bonding” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tasks that build a flair for simple language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The activity slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1296s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:36</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1514s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:14</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A note inviting your friends to a party when college reopens after nearly a year of lockdown.</li>\n<li>Captions of no more than 5 words for two photos. “Women by the river” or “poor boy” is the easy answer: “can there be more?” Think differently from the crowd (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A new and witty birthday greeting; an invitation to your college teachers to your wedding; an invitation to your friends for a weekend walk to another city (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Complete: “God created friends to make up…”, “Home is …”, “More money …”, “Real power…”, “Beauty lies…” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A paragraph of about 150 words: home-cooked food vs door-delivered food; a dream holiday; what I like about online classes; social media, good or bad.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The final mantra: “practice, revision, reading, and then revision again” of whatever you write (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Note-writing</strong> (11.1): plan a synopsis first: when you got it, the physical description (colour, shape, size, weight), special features, what you like best, any event. First draft: everything; final draft: “all and only” the needed facts (385 words in 21 sentences → 150 words in 12). Be factual. Choose the accurate word: <em>can expand to</em>, <em>smaller</em> (comparing two things), <em>like</em> (not <em>prefer</em> without a second thing), <em>parts</em>, <em>a pocket</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Business writing</strong> (11.2): facts (what, when, where, how much, for how much, how many); a fixed form (from, to, date, salutation, subject, body, closing); a rank order (the most important first). Correct English is not enough: the lost-bag letter needs the bus, the time, the stops, the bag and what is inside, not feelings.</p>\n<p><strong>Instructions</strong> (11.3): sequence, directions, numbers, options; clear, unambiguous, precise, brief. Write the steps in the order they happen; give names and numbers (Exit 1), not left and right; say the ticket and the token, the lines, the changes, the time. Addresses do not count in the word limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Personal details in a business letter</strong> (11.4): ask in advance; business-like, formal and polite. The first sentence says where, how and when you arrive; then the room, food, sightseeing and shopping, in order of importance. Cut boasting, feelings and the obvious.</p>\n<p><strong>Data reports</strong> (11.5, 11.6): numbers make writing objective. Say (a) what the data are about and the period, (b) how they are presented, (c) the main trends, (d) significant details and differences between nodes, (e) a concluding remark. About 150 words (5 more or less). Use chunks (<em>over half</em>, <em>one fourth</em>) and <em>over/under</em> for <em>more than/less than</em>. Say what the chart does not tell. No mechanical copying, no tangents; check every number.</p>\n<p><strong>Essays</strong> (11.7): an essay tests language, knowledge of the world and the order of importance. It needs content (arguments, examples, data, evidence), planning (a synopsis, a chain of points) and examples. Cut irrelevant ideas, clichés (<em>the pathetic state of…</em>), vague words (<em>tremendous</em>), doubles (<em>roles and responsibilities</em>) and piles of pronouns (repeat the noun). Revise with a dictionary and a thesaurus.</p>\n<p><strong>Simplicity</strong> (11.8): simple language is understood, remembered and survives (Buddha, Shakespeare, Wordsworth, Dickens, Hawking). Formal language for the public; chat jargon (<em>LOL</em>, <em>ROFL</em>) only among friends. Five rules: at most 10 words a sentence, one verb, words of one or two syllables, more than one draft, a dictionary and thesaurus.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Accurate or not? Fix each phrase from the phone note and give the reason. (a) “It can be expanded up to 256GB.” (b) “a more powerful processor, but very small power back up” (c) “I do not prefer phones that require frequent charging for phones.” (d) “its touch screen, camera, and other extras” (e) “I keep it in pant pockets.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>“It can expand to 256GB.”</strong> The passive <em>be expanded up to</em> adds words and nothing else.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>“but a smaller power back up.”</strong> The sentence compares two phones, so it needs the comparative <em>smaller</em>: the old phone’s backup was smaller than the new one’s.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>“I do not like phones that need frequent charging.”</strong> <em>Prefer</em> needs two things (<em>prefer A to B</em>), and here there is only one. And “phones ... for phones” says the same word twice.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>“and other parts.”</strong> A screen and a camera are parts of the phone, not extras.</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>“in a pant pocket”</strong> (or “in a pocket of my pants”). One phone cannot be in two pockets.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">You left your wallet on a city bus yesterday. Which of these belong in your letter to the depot manager, and in what order? (1) The wallet was a gift from your grandfather. (2) The bus number and the time you got on. (3) The stops where you got on and off. (4) You could not sleep last night. (5) The wallet’s colour, material and brand. (6) What is inside: which cards, about how much cash. (7) A long apology for troubling him. (The situation is mine, built on the lecture’s lost-bag letter.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Keep</strong> (2), (3), (5) and (6): they are facts that let the office tell your wallet from dozens of others.</li>\n<li><strong>Drop</strong> (1) and (4): feelings do not help anyone find a wallet. “The reader can pity you”, but cannot find it faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Drop</strong> (7): one short polite request is enough, like the lecture’s “Please, be good enough to help me find it.”</li>\n<li><strong>The order:</strong> From, To, Date, “Sir,” and the subject line (“Lost wallet - Reg.”); then the journey, (2) and (3); then the request; then the wallet, (5); then its contents, (6); then a short close.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is wrong with these directions? Use the four parameters of Lecture 11.3. “Take the metro to the big station and change there. Before that buy a token. Get down at my station and turn left. It takes 13 or 30 minutes.” (The directions are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sequence:</strong> “Take the metro … Before that, buy a token”: the steps are out of order. Write them as they happen: buy the token, enter the platform, take the train.</li>\n<li><strong>Options:</strong> “the big station” and “change there”: which station? Which line (which colour), towards which end? Name them.</li>\n<li><strong>Directions:</strong> “turn left”: whose left? Use a number: “Take Exit 1”.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers:</strong> “13 or 30 minutes” confuses the reader. Give one clear figure, such as “about 30 minutes”.</li>\n<li><strong>Also missing:</strong> what to do with the token (keep it, or you cannot get out) and “do not leave the station” where you change.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Match each sentence of the Facebook final draft to a point of a data report, (a) to (f). (1) “The table shows the number of Facebook users and the frequency of their use.” (2) “The left hand column presents frequency of use, and the one on the right gives numbers.” (3) “Asia accounts for nearly 40% of the daily active users of Facebook.” (4) “...women exceed men by over 10% in the number of friends.” (5) “World is fast turning into a global village.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(1) → <strong>(a)</strong> what it is all about; the next sentence adds the period, January 2018.</li>\n<li>(2) → <strong>(b)</strong> how it is presented: a table, with what is in each column.</li>\n<li>(3) → <strong>(c)</strong> a main trend: a large share of the daily users is in Asia (by the table, <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">499</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36%</span></span></span></span>).</span></span></li>\n<li>(4) → <strong>(d)</strong> and <strong>(e)</strong>: a significant detail, and a difference between two groups (men and women).</li>\n<li>(5) → <strong>(f)</strong> the concluding remark.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Use the household-survey chart (housewife/unemployed 56%, domestic help 28%, tradesperson 7%, other service job 5%, self-employed 2%, labourer 2%). (a) Say 56% and 28% as chunks. (b) What share of the women are in the other five slices? (c) Among those women, what share are domestic help? Show each step.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) 56% is more than 50%, so it is <strong>“over half”</strong>. 28% is a little more than 25%, which is one fourth, so it is <strong>“a little more than one fourth”</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) The other five slices are everything except the first one:\n<div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">56</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">44</span></span></span></span></span></div>\nso 44% of the women are in the other five slices.</p>\n<p>(c) Domestic help is 28 of those 44:\n<div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">44</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">28</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">11</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.64</span></span></span></span></span></div></p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> about 64%, nearly two-thirds. A report can say: “Over half the women are housewives or unemployed; among the rest, nearly two-thirds work as domestic help.” That is why the final draft’s “most women work as domestic help” is true only of the women outside the first slice. (Parts (b) and (c) are my arithmetic.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Rewrite this sentence with the rules of Lecture 11.8 (short sentences, one verb each, short words): “Since all the members of most families are so extremely busy these days, they hardly ever get any opportunity to sit, work and be together, which is unfortunate.” (The sentence is mine, built on the lecture’s Draft 1.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Count:</strong> 28 words in one sentence, with many verbs (<em>are, get, sit, work, be, is</em>). Far over the limit of 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Split it by its ideas:</strong> (1) families are busy; (2) they are seldom together.</li>\n<li><strong>Cut the empty words:</strong> <em>so extremely</em>, <em>any opportunity to</em>, <em>which is unfortunate</em> (the reader can judge that).</li>\n<li><strong>Shorter words:</strong> <em>opportunity</em> has five syllables (op-por-tu-ni-ty), and it is not needed at all.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “These days families are busy. They are hardly ever together.” Two sentences of 5 words each, with one verb each (<em>are</em>, <em>are</em>). This is close to the lecture’s Draft 2: “They are hardly ever together.”</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
